  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chilly overnight Tuesday night. Chance for \nfrost in the north. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nCool airmass moves over northern Maine Tuesday night. There is \na chance for atmospheric decoupling early in the night, as skies\nclear out. This will help temperatures to drop into the 30s. \nPotential for northern areas in Aroostook to drop into the low-\nto-mid 30s, which will lead to some frost development. Frost \nchances are going to be highly dependent on the timing of some \nhigh level clouds moving in late Tuesday night into early \nWednesday morning. If the clouds move in earlier in the night, \ntemperatures will stabilize faster. If clouds move in slower, \nthen there is more time for the temperatures to drop with clear \nskies. Currently, the set up looks more favorable for \ndecoupling, and thus colder temperatures. \n\nThe next chance of frost comes Friday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Soaking rain is expected areawide Wednesday night,\nwith gusty winds Wednesday night and Thursday. Can't rule out \nisolated power outages during the day Thursday from the wind.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nFairly strong system expected Wednesday night/Thursday. Surface\nlow pressure will be passing well to our north, with a potent\nwarm front Wednesday night followed by a cold front Thursday. A\nfew impressive things about this system include an unusually\nstrong upper level westerly jet stream, high precipitable water,\nand unusually strong 850mb winds. These three parameters are\nrunning above the 95th percentile for this time of year.\n\nRain: Rain looks to begin early Wednesday evening and taper off\naround sunrise Thursday. The rain will be occasionally moderate,\nand can't rule out an embedded thunderstorm with some\ninstability aloft. We are looking at over 0.25 inch rain for \nmost areas, with a 50-75 percent chance of exceeding 0.50 inch. \nChances are only 20 percent or less of exceeding 1 inch though.\nThis won't be enough rain for flooding concerns, but will help \nwith the dry conditions/drought. Could be a few showers\npersisting during the day Thursday mainly in the north, but the\nvast majority of the rain comes Wednesday night.\n\nWind: As mentioned before, we are looking at potent winds aloft,\nmost likely coming in the middle of the night Wednesday night\nfrom the south, and again during the day Thursday from the west.\nThe winds aloft will be the strongest Wednesday night with up \nto about 55 kt at 850mb, but it is very unlikely that these \nwinds will reach the ground due to stable low levels that are \ndecoupled from the atmosphere aloft. More realistically, we are\nlooking at gusts as high as about 30 mph from the south \nWednesday night. During the day Thursday, though the winds aloft\naren't quite as strong, the winds have a much better shot at \nmixing down to the ground thanks to cold advection aloft with \nsteeper lapse rates, plus the fact that it's daytime. Gusts to \nabout 35 mph from the west appear likely Thursday, and with wet \nsoils after Wednesday night's rain plus leaves on the trees, \nthere could be very isolated tree damage/isolated power outages.\nIt remains breezy Thursday night/Friday, though not quite as\nmuch as Thursday.",
